## Command & Control Architecture (Conceptual Overview)

A C2 framework provides structured communication between an operator and compromised hosts. Understanding the architecture helps defenders recognize the traffic patterns.

### Core Components

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                      C2 ARCHITECTURE                         │
├─────────────────┬─────────────────┬─────────────────────────┤
│    OPERATOR     │      SERVER     │      IMPLANT (Agent)    │
├─────────────────┼─────────────────┼─────────────────────────┤
│ • Issue cmds    │ • Listen/beacon │ • Poll for tasks        │
│ • View output   │ • Queue tasks   │ • Execute & return data │
│ • Manage hosts  │ • Log activity  │ • Persist on host       │
└─────────────────┴─────────────────┴─────────────────────────┘
```

### The Command Loop (Conceptual Flow)

The "heartbeat" of a C2 is the polling mechanism:

```
IMPLANT BEHAVIOR PATTERN:
1. Sleep(interval)
2. HTTP GET to server/checkin
3. Receive encrypted task (or "none")
4. If task exists:
   a. Execute command locally
   b. Capture stdout/stderr
   c. POST results to server/output
5. Goto step 1
```

This loop creates a recognizable traffic pattern—regular intervals of GET/POST requests to the same endpoint.

